window.__hvBdRegister("homer-iliad", {"key":"homer-iliad","sections":[{"i":0,"j":31,"loc":"Book I (1 of 2)","title":"The plague and the quarrel over Briseis","summary":"The poet invokes the goddess to sing the anger of Achilles. Agamemnon rudely refuses the ransom Chryses offers for his daughter, and Apollo answers the priest's prayer with nine days of plague. When Calchas, under Achilles' protection, names the cause, Agamemnon agrees to return Chryseis but demands another prize, and the quarrel ends with his threat to take Achilles' own prize, Briseis. Minerva stops Achilles from drawing his sword; he swears on the sceptre that the Achaeans will miss him when Hector cuts them down, and Nestor's attempt at mediation fails. Ulysses sails Chryseis home while the heralds come to Achilles' tent for Briseis and he bids Patroclus hand her over.","ideas":["Agamemnon's insult to Apollo's priest brings the plague","The dispute is over prizes of honour awarded by the army","Minerva restrains Achilles from killing Agamemnon","Achilles' oath on the sceptre to withdraw from the fighting"],"terms":[{"t":"geras (prize of honour)","d":"A share of spoil given by the army as a mark of honour, like Chryseis and Briseis; losing one is a public dishonour."},{"t":"hecatomb","d":"A great sacrifice of cattle, here the offering needed to appease Apollo."}]},{"i":32,"j":55,"loc":"Book I (2 of 2)","title":"Thetis pleads with Jove; strife on Olympus","summary":"Achilles weeps by the sea and tells his mother Thetis what happened, asking her to beg Jove to give the Trojans victory until the Achaeans rue the insult. Thetis laments his short and sorrowful life and promises to go when the gods return from the Ethiopians. Meanwhile Ulysses restores Chryseis, Chryses prays for the plague to end, and the sacrifice and feast are described in full. Twelve days later Thetis clasps Jove's knees, and he grants her request with his solemn nod that shakes Olympus. Juno guesses the plan and quarrels with him until Jove threatens her, and Vulcan restores peace by serving nectar and recalling his fall to Lemnos.","ideas":["Achilles is fated to a short life, which Thetis mourns","Jove's nod is his most binding promise","The sacrifice at Chryse appeases Apollo","The gods' quarrel ends in laughter and feasting"],"terms":[{"t":"suppliant gesture","d":"Clasping the knees and touching the chin of the one entreated, as Thetis does to Jove."}]},{"i":56,"j":88,"loc":"Book II (1 of 2)","title":"The false dream, the test, and Thersites","summary":"Jove sends a lying dream in Nestor's likeness telling Agamemnon he will take Troy now. Agamemnon tells the elders he will test the army by proposing flight, and the people rush for the ships. Juno sends Minerva to Ulysses, who uses Agamemnon's sceptre to coax the chiefs and beat the commoners back to the assembly. Thersites rails at Agamemnon and is struck and silenced by Ulysses, to the army's amusement. Ulysses recalls Calchas' omen at Aulis of nine years' war and victory in the tenth, Nestor urges marshalling by tribes and clans, and after a sacrifice the host, roused by Minerva's aegis, pours onto the plain in a run of similes.","ideas":["Jove deceives Agamemnon with a dream","The test of the army nearly ends the war","Thersites, the ugly railer, is beaten into silence","The Aulis omen of the serpent and sparrows"],"terms":[{"t":"skēptron (sceptre)","d":"Agamemnon's inherited staff, made by Vulcan and passed from Jove, the sign of his authority."}]},{"i":89,"j":140,"loc":"Book II (2 of 2)","title":"The Catalogue of Ships and the Trojan forces","summary":"The poet calls on the Muses and lists the Achaean contingents, region by region, with their leaders and number of ships, from the Boeotians to the Magnetes. Brief digressions mark Thamyris maimed by the Muses, the absent Achilles grieving for Briseis, the dead Protesilaus, and Philoctetes left wounded on Lemnos. The best horses are Eumelus' mares and the best man, while Achilles sulks, is Ajax son of Telamon. Iris, in the form of Polites, warns the Trojans, and Hector leads them out; the Trojan and allied contingents are then listed, from Hector's Trojans and Aeneas' Dardanians to Sarpedon and Glaucus of Lycia.","ideas":["The Catalogue of Ships","Achilles' Myrmidons stand idle at the ships","The Trojan allies speak many tongues","Foreshadowed deaths at Achilles' hands in the river"],"terms":[{"t":"Muses","d":"Goddesses who see all things and are invoked so the poet can name what he knows only by report."}]},{"i":141,"j":183,"loc":"Book III","title":"Paris and Menelaus duel; Helen on the wall","summary":"Paris challenges the Achaeans but shrinks from Menelaus until Hector shames him, and he then proposes a single combat for Helen and her wealth. Iris summons Helen to the Scaean gates, where the elders marvel at her beauty and she names Agamemnon, Ulysses, Ajax and Idomeneus for Priam, wondering why her brothers are missing. Priam and Agamemnon swear the oaths over the sacrifice. Menelaus wins the duel, but his sword breaks and Venus snaps the helmet strap and carries Paris to his bedchamber. Helen rebukes the goddess, is cowed by her threat, scorns Paris and then goes to bed with him, while Agamemnon claims victory for Menelaus.","ideas":["Hector rebukes Paris as handsome but cowardly","The view from the wall (teichoskopia): Helen names the Achaean chiefs","The oath-sacrifice binds both sides","Venus snatches Paris from death"],"terms":[{"t":"horkia (oaths)","d":"The sworn covenant, sealed with lambs and wine, that the duel will settle the war."}]},{"i":184,"j":201,"loc":"Book IV (1 of 2)","title":"Pandarus breaks the truce","summary":"On Olympus Jove teases Juno and Minerva and suggests peace; Juno insists on Troy's ruin, and they bargain: he may later destroy her favourite cities, and Minerva is to make the Trojans break the oaths. Disguised as Laodocus, Minerva persuades Pandarus to shoot Menelaus. She turns the arrow so it only grazes him through belt and cuirass. Agamemnon laments, predicting Troy's punishment for the broken oath and his own disgrace should Menelaus die, and sends for Machaon, who draws the arrow and applies healing drugs.","ideas":["Juno trades her own cities for Troy's destruction","Minerva tempts Pandarus to break the oath","The wound of Menelaus as a pledge of Troy's guilt","Agamemnon trusts Jove to punish the oath-breakers"],"terms":[{"t":"oath-breaking","d":"The Trojans' violation of the sworn truce, which Agamemnon says Jove will surely avenge."}]},{"i":202,"j":224,"loc":"Book IV (2 of 2)","title":"Agamemnon reviews the army; battle is joined","summary":"As the Trojans advance, Agamemnon goes on foot among the troops, praising the eager and rebuking the slack. He honours Idomeneus and the two Ajaxes, admires Nestor's tactics, and taunts Menestheus and Ulysses, then Diomed with the deeds of his father Tydeus at Thebes; Sthenelus retorts, but Diomed accepts the rebuke. The silent Achaeans and clamorous Trojans meet, urged on by Minerva, Mars and Strife. Antilochus kills Echepolus, Ajax kills young Simoeisius, Ulysses avenges Leucus, and many fall on both sides.","ideas":["The review of the troops (epipolesis)","Nestor's order of battle and chariot tactics","Tydeus held up as a model for Diomed","The first killings and the simile of the felled poplar"],"terms":[{"t":"Eris (Strife)","d":"Sister of Mars, small at first but growing till her head reaches heaven, who spreads discord in battle."}]},{"i":225,"j":261,"loc":"Book V (1 of 2)","title":"Diomed's rampage and the wounding of Venus","summary":"Minerva fills Diomed with valour and a blaze of fire, and he cuts through the Trojans; Minerva leads Mars out of the battle. Wounded by Pandarus, Diomed prays to Minerva, who heals him, lets him tell gods from men, and permits him to strike Venus only. He kills Pandarus and crushes Aeneas' hip with a stone; Sthenelus takes Aeneas' famous horses. Diomed wounds Venus in the hand as she carries Aeneas away, and Apollo takes over the rescue. Venus flees to Olympus in Mars' chariot, where Dione comforts her with tales of gods hurt by mortals, and Jove tells her to leave war alone.","ideas":["Diomed's aristeia, with Minerva at his side","Pandarus killed; Aeneas saved by Venus","A mortal wounds a goddess and draws ichor","Dione's stories of gods suffering at men's hands"],"terms":[{"t":"aristeia","d":"A hero's spell of supreme prowess in battle, here Diomed's."},{"t":"ichor","d":"The immortal fluid in the gods' veins, since they eat no bread and drink no wine."}]},{"i":262,"j":293,"loc":"Book V (2 of 2)","title":"Mars rallies Troy; Diomed wounds Mars","summary":"Apollo beats Diomed back from Aeneas, heals him in Pergamus and sends Mars to rouse the Trojans. Sarpedon rebukes Hector, Aeneas returns, and with Mars beside Hector the Achaeans give ground; Sarpedon kills Tlepolemus, son of Hercules, and is himself wounded in the thigh. Juno and Minerva drive down in their chariot with Jove's leave; Juno shouts like Stentor, and Minerva shames Diomed and takes the reins herself. Wearing the helmet of Hades, she drives Diomed's spear into Mars, who bellows and flees to Olympus. Jove scolds him as the most hateful of the gods but has Paeeon heal him.","ideas":["Diomed yields to Apollo's warning not to fight gods","Sarpedon against Tlepolemus, two sons of Jove's line","Minerva and Diomed wound Mars","Jove's contempt for the war-god"],"terms":[{"t":"aegis","d":"Minerva's tasselled goatskin bearing Rout, Strife, Panic and the Gorgon's head."}]},{"i":294,"j":312,"loc":"Book VI (1 of 2)","title":"Glaucus and Diomed exchange armour","summary":"With the gods gone, the Achaeans press hard; Agamemnon persuades Menelaus to kill the suppliant Adrestus, sparing no Trojan. Helenus sends Hector to the city to have the women offer a robe to Minerva. Diomed meets Glaucus and asks if he is a god, recalling Lycurgus' fate. Glaucus answers that men's generations are like leaves, then tells of his grandfather Bellerophon, slandered by Antea, sent to Lycia, and victor over the Chimaera, the Solymi and the Amazons. Diomed finds their grandfathers were guest-friends, and they exchange armour, Glaucus foolishly giving gold for bronze.","ideas":["Agamemnon refuses all mercy to the Trojans","Generations of men are like leaves","The story of Bellerophon","Guest-friendship outweighs the enmity of war"],"terms":[{"t":"xenia (guest-friendship)","d":"The hereditary bond of host and guest, which makes Diomed and Glaucus refuse to fight."}]},{"i":313,"j":330,"loc":"Book VI (2 of 2)","title":"Hector in Troy; farewell to Andromache","summary":"Hector reaches Priam's palace, refuses the wine Hecuba offers, and sends her with the matrons to lay a robe on Minerva's knees; Theano prays, but the goddess refuses. He rebukes Paris, who agrees to arm, and declines Helen's invitation to rest as she reproaches herself. At the Scaean gates Andromache, whose family Achilles killed, begs him to stay on the wall. Hector answers that shame forbids it, foresees Troy's fall and her captivity, and prays over Astyanax, who is frightened by his helmet. The women mourn him as if already dead, and Paris, bright in armour, overtakes him.","ideas":["Minerva rejects the Trojan women's offering","Helen's self-reproach and Paris's idleness","Hector's farewell to Andromache and Astyanax","Hector knows Troy will fall but chooses honour"],"terms":[{"t":"aidōs (shame)","d":"Regard for others' judgment, which keeps Hector from shirking battle."},{"t":"Astyanax","d":"\"Lord of the city,\" the people's name for Hector's son, since his father alone guarded Ilius."}]},{"i":331,"j":353,"loc":"Book VII (1 of 2)","title":"Hector challenges the Achaeans; duel with Ajax","summary":"Hector and Alexandrus return to the field, and Minerva and Apollo agree to halt the general fighting by having Hector challenge a champion; Helenus divines their plan and tells Hector. Hector offers single combat on terms that the loser's body be returned for burial, but the Achaeans sit silent until Menelaus volunteers and Agamemnon restrains him. Nestor shames them with the tale of his youthful victory over Ereuthalion, nine chiefs rise, and the lot falls to Ajax. Ajax and Hector exchange spear casts and stones, Hector is wounded and knocked down, and the heralds part them at nightfall; the two exchange gifts, a sword and a purple girdle.","ideas":["Apollo and Minerva arrange the duel to pause the battle","Nestor's reproach rouses nine volunteers; the lot picks Ajax","Ajax's sevenfold oxhide shield withstands Hector's spear","The fighters part in friendship with exchanged gifts"],"terms":[{"t":"lot (klēros)","d":"Each volunteer marks a token and throws it into Agamemnon's helmet; Nestor shakes it out to choose the champion."},{"t":"heralds","d":"Talthybius and Idaeus, messengers of gods and men, part the combatants with their staves because night is falling."}]},{"i":354,"j":367,"loc":"Book VII (2 of 2)","title":"Truce for the dead and the Achaean wall","summary":"Agamemnon honours Ajax at a feast, and Nestor proposes burning the dead and building a wall and trench around the ships. In the Trojan council Antenor urges giving Helen back, but Alexandrus will return only the treasure; Idaeus carries the offer to the Achaeans, Diomed rejects it, and Agamemnon grants a truce to burn the dead. Both sides burn their dead in silence, and the Achaeans build the barrow, wall, gates and staked trench. Neptune complains that the wall was built without hecatombs, and Jove tells him he may destroy it once the Achaeans sail; the night ends in feasting on Lemnian wine while Jove thunders ominously.","ideas":["Paris refuses to give up Helen but offers the treasure","Diomed: the Trojans' doom is at hand, take nothing","The dead are burned during a truce","The Achaean wall is built without offerings to the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"hecatomb","d":"A great sacrifice; Neptune objects that the Achaeans built their wall without offering any."}]},{"i":368,"j":397,"loc":"Book VIII (1 of 2)","title":"Jove forbids the gods; the Achaeans routed","summary":"Jove forbids every god to help either side, boasting that he could haul them all up on a golden chain, and goes to Ida to watch. At midday he weighs the fates in his golden scales, the Achaean scale sinks, and his lightning drives them back. Diomed rescues Nestor, whose horse Paris has shot, but Jove's thunderbolt forces them to retreat amid Hector's taunts. Agamemnon's prayer brings an eagle omen and a brief rally in which Teucer shoots many Trojans from behind Ajax's shield, until Hector strikes him down with a stone and drives the Achaeans back over the trench.","ideas":["Jove's golden chain shows his supremacy over the gods","The golden scales turn the day against the Achaeans","Diomed's rescue of Nestor ends in forced retreat","Teucer's archery rally is stopped by Hector's stone"],"terms":[{"t":"golden scales","d":"Jove places two fates of death in his balance, and the Achaeans' scale sinks to earth."}]},{"i":398,"j":410,"loc":"Book VIII (2 of 2)","title":"Juno and Minerva turned back; Trojan watchfires","summary":"Juno and Minerva arm and drive out to help the Achaeans, Minerva angry that Jove favours Thetis' request. Jove sends Iris to threaten them with lamed horses and lightning wounds, and they turn back. On Olympus Jove foretells that Hector will not stop until Achilles is roused in the fight over the body of Patroclus at the ships. At nightfall Hector camps on the plain, orders watchfires and provisions so the Achaeans cannot slip away by sea, and boasts that he will face Diomed in the morning; a thousand fires burn like stars between the ships and the Xanthus.","ideas":["Jove's threat through Iris halts the two goddesses","Jove's prophecy of the fight over Patroclus' body","Hector camps on the plain to keep the Achaeans from fleeing","The simile of the watchfires as stars on a still night"],"terms":[{"t":"Iris","d":"Jove's swift messenger, who repeats his threats word for word to Juno and Minerva."}]},{"i":411,"j":429,"loc":"Book IX (1 of 2)","title":"Agamemnon's remorse and the embassy's offer","summary":"In despair Agamemnon proposes sailing home; Diomed rebukes him and vows to fight on, and Nestor has sentinels posted and calls a council. At Agamemnon's feast Nestor reminds him that he dishonoured Achilles by taking Briseis, and Agamemnon admits his folly and lists a vast atonement: tripods, gold, horses, Lesbian women, Briseis untouched, Trojan spoil, a daughter in marriage and seven cities. Nestor chooses Phoenix, Ajax and Ulysses as envoys. They find Achilles singing to the lyre with Patroclus, are hospitably fed, and Ulysses describes the Achaeans' danger, recalls Peleus' advice to curb his temper, and repeats the offered gifts.","ideas":["Diomed defies Agamemnon's proposal to flee","Agamemnon admits he was blinded by passion","The catalogue of gifts offered to Achilles","Achilles is found singing the feats of heroes"],"terms":[{"t":"atonement gifts","d":"The goods Agamemnon offers to make amends and win back Achilles."}]},{"i":430,"j":446,"loc":"Book IX (2 of 2)","title":"Achilles refuses; Phoenix's plea and Meleager","summary":"Achilles rejects the offer: the brave and the coward receive the same honour, Agamemnon kept the spoils and took his prize, and no wealth can buy back a life. He reports Thetis' prophecy of two fates, short life with lasting fame or long life without it, and says he will sail home. Phoenix, his old tutor, recalls his own flight from his father's curse and his care of the child Achilles, describes the Prayers who follow after sin, and tells how Meleager held out in anger and saved Calydon too late to receive the gifts. Achilles keeps Phoenix with him, and to Ajax's blunt reproach replies that he will fight only when Hector reaches the Myrmidon ships; Ulysses reports the refusal, and Diomed urges battle at dawn.","ideas":["Achilles' choice between fame with early death and long obscure life","Phoenix's allegory of the Prayers as daughters of Jove","The Meleager story as a warning against delayed help","Achilles yields only to the point of fighting when his own ships burn"],"terms":[{"t":"two fates","d":"Thetis' prophecy that Achilles may die at Troy with undying fame or go home to a long life without renown."},{"t":"Litai (Prayers)","d":"Phoenix's lame, wrinkled daughters of Jove who follow sin and heal the harm it does."}]},{"i":447,"j":473,"loc":"Book X (1 of 2)","title":"Sleepless chiefs and the spying mission","summary":"Agamemnon cannot sleep and, with Menelaus, rouses Nestor, who wakes Ulysses, Diomed and others; they inspect the watch and find the sentinels alert. In council beyond the trench Nestor asks for a volunteer to spy on the Trojans for great reward. Diomed offers, wanting a companion because two see more than one, and chooses Ulysses as the man Minerva loves. The two arm, Ulysses in a boar's-tusk helmet with a long history, hear a heron sent by Minerva, pray to her, and go out among the dead like lions.","ideas":["Nestor's image of life and death on a razor's edge","Diomed chooses Ulysses for his quickness of mind","The boar's-tusk helmet and its line of owners","Minerva's heron omen and the spies' prayers"],"terms":[{"t":"boar's-tusk helmet","d":"A leather helmet studded with boar's teeth, stolen by Autolycus and passed down until Meriones lends it to Ulysses."}]},{"i":474,"j":496,"loc":"Book X (2 of 2)","title":"Dolon captured; Rhesus and his horses","summary":"Hector offers Achilles' own horses to any Trojan who will spy on the ships, and Dolon, a swift runner, takes the oath and sets out in a wolfskin. Ulysses and Diomed let him pass, run him down, and question him; he reveals where Hector and the allies lie and describes the newly arrived Thracian king Rhesus and his snow-white horses. Diomed kills Dolon despite his plea for ransom, and Ulysses dedicates his spoils to Minerva. Diomed slays twelve sleeping Thracians and Rhesus while Ulysses drives off the horses; warned by Minerva they return, and are welcomed by Nestor before bathing and pouring offerings to Minerva.","ideas":["Hector's rash oath tempts Dolon","Dolon betrays the layout of the Trojan camp","The night slaughter of Rhesus and twelve Thracians","Minerva guides the Achaean pair; Apollo rouses the Thracians too late"],"terms":[{"t":"Rhesus","d":"Thracian king newly arrived at Troy, with horses whiter than snow and golden armour."}]},{"i":497,"j":516,"loc":"Book XI (1 of 3)","title":"Agamemnon's day of valour and his wound","summary":"Jove sends Discord to rouse the Achaeans, and Agamemnon arms in an elaborate breastplate and Gorgon shield. After an even morning the Danaans break the Trojan ranks at midday, and Agamemnon kills Bienor, Oileus, Isus and Antiphus, and the sons of Antimachus, rejecting their pleas for ransom, as he drives the Trojans to the Scaean gates. Jove sends Iris to tell Hector to hold back until Agamemnon is wounded. Agamemnon kills Iphidamas but is speared in the arm by Coon, whom he also kills; when the pain grows he withdraws to the ships.","ideas":["Discord alone among the gods is present at the battle","Agamemnon's arming and his pitiless slaughter","Jove's instruction to Hector to wait for Agamemnon's wound","The pathos of Iphidamas, slain far from his new bride"],"terms":[{"t":"aristeia","d":"A hero's run of outstanding deeds in battle; here Agamemnon's, cut short by his wound."}]},{"i":517,"j":541,"loc":"Book XI (2 of 3)","title":"Hector's onslaught and the Achaean chiefs wounded","summary":"With Agamemnon gone, Hector kills many Achaean chiefs until Ulysses and Diomed make a stand. Diomed's spear strikes Hector's helmet and stuns him, but Paris shoots Diomed through the foot from behind the tomb of Ilus and he leaves the field. Alone, Ulysses resolves to hold his ground, kills several Trojans including Socus, and is wounded until Menelaus and Ajax rescue him. Paris wounds the physician Machaon, whom Nestor carries off; Jove fills Ajax with fear, and he retreats slowly like a lion or a stubborn ass, until Eurypylus, himself wounded by Paris, rallies the Achaeans to cover him.","ideas":["Paris the archer wounds Diomed, Machaon and Eurypylus","Ulysses' soliloquy: the brave man stands firm","A physician is worth many other men","Ajax's stubborn retreat under Jove's sent fear"],"terms":[{"t":"Machaon","d":"Son of Aesculapius and a physician of the Achaeans; his wound sets Nestor's chariot driving past Achilles."}]},{"i":542,"j":553,"loc":"Book XI (3 of 3)","title":"Patroclus sent to Nestor; Nestor's long speech","summary":"Achilles, watching from his ship, sends Patroclus to learn who is wounded in Nestor's chariot, the narrator noting this as the beginning of Patroclus' ruin. Nestor and Machaon refresh themselves with Hecamede's mixed drink, and Nestor tells Patroclus at length of his youthful exploits in the cattle raid on Elis and the battle against the Epeans. He reminds Patroclus of Menoetius' charge to counsel Achilles, and suggests that if Achilles will not fight he send Patroclus in his armour so the Trojans mistake him for Achilles. Returning, Patroclus meets the wounded Eurypylus and stops to cut out the arrow and dress the wound with a healing herb.","ideas":["The errand that starts Patroclus' fate","Nestor's tale of his triumph over the Epeans","The plan for Patroclus to fight in Achilles' armour","Patroclus tends Eurypylus with herbs learned from Chiron"],"terms":[{"t":"Hecamede","d":"The woman awarded to Nestor from Tenedos, who prepares the cheese-and-wine drink in his golden cup."}]},{"i":554,"j":564,"loc":"Book XII (1 of 2)","title":"The doomed wall and the assault begins","summary":"The narrator foretells that after Troy's fall Neptune and Apollo will turn the rivers of Ida against the Achaean wall and wash it away, because it was built without offerings. Hector urges the Trojans across the trench, but the horses balk, and Polydamas advises leaving the chariots and attacking on foot in five companies. Asius alone drives his chariot at an open gate on the left, where the Lapiths Polypoetes and Leonteus stand like oaks and defend it. Asius complains that Jove has lied to him, but the two keep killing Trojans as stones rain from the wall.","ideas":["The future destruction of the wall by rivers and Neptune","Polydamas' counsel to fight on foot","Asius' folly in keeping his chariot","The Lapith pair hold the gate"],"terms":[{"t":"Lapithae","d":"A Thessalian people; Polypoetes son of Pirithous and Leonteus are their champions at the gate."}]},{"i":565,"j":578,"loc":"Book XII (2 of 2)","title":"Eagle omen, Sarpedon's speech, Hector breaks gate","summary":"An eagle drops a struggling snake among the Trojans, and Polydamas reads it as a warning that they will not return in good order; Hector scorns bird omens, declaring that the one best omen is fighting for one's country. Jove sends a dust storm and the Trojans tear at the battlements while the two Ajaxes rally the defenders. Sarpedon tells Glaucus that princes must earn their honours by fighting in front, since death cannot be escaped, and leads the Lycians against Menestheus' tower; Ajax and Teucer come to help, Glaucus is wounded, and Sarpedon tears down a battlement. The fight hangs even until Hector smashes the gate with a huge stone, leaps through, and the Danaans flee to their ships.","ideas":["Hector rejects Polydamas' reading of the omen","Sarpedon's speech on rank, duty and mortality","The battle balanced like a woman's wool-scales","Hector bursts the gate and the wall is breached"],"terms":[{"t":"omen (oiōnos)","d":"A sign from birds; Hector says the best is to fight for one's country."}]},{"i":579,"j":606,"loc":"Book XIII (1 of 2)","title":"Neptune secretly rallies the Achaeans","summary":"With Jove's gaze turned away from Troy, Neptune drives his chariot over the sea to the Achaean camp and, in the likeness of Calchas, fills the two Ajaxes with strength; Ajax son of Oileus recognises a god by his gait. Neptune shames the younger captains, who form a close wall of shields that halts Hector's charge. Teucer kills Imbrius, Hector kills Amphimachus, Neptune's grandson, and the lesser Ajax hurls Imbrius' severed head at Hector's feet. Angered, Neptune takes the form of Thoas and rouses Idomeneus, who meets Meriones returning for a spear; after Idomeneus describes how ambush shows the coward and the brave, the two go to fight on the left wing.","ideas":["Neptune helps the Achaeans in disguise because he dare not openly defy his elder brother Jove","The Achaeans' serried 'living fence' of shields stops Hector like a rolling boulder on the level","Idomeneus' portrait of the coward and the brave man in ambush","Even cowards gain courage from companionship, says the disguised god"],"terms":[{"t":"Neptune (Poseidon)","d":"God of the sea and earthquake, Jove's brother, who here takes human shapes to encourage the Achaeans against Jove's plan."},{"t":"Idomeneus","d":"Grey-haired leader of the Cretans, grandson of Minos, whose deeds dominate this book."}]},{"i":607,"j":634,"loc":"Book XIII (2 of 2)","title":"Idomeneus' deeds and Hector at the ships","summary":"The narrator explains that Jove and Neptune have tied a knot of war that neither side can loosen. Idomeneus kills Othryoneus, who had been promised Cassandra, mocks him, then kills Asius and Alcathous; Deiphobus calls on Aeneas, and a hard fight follows in which Ascalaphus son of Mars falls and Deiphobus is wounded. Menelaus wounds Helenus, kills Pisander and complains that the Trojans never tire of battle; Paris kills Euchenor, who had chosen death at Troy over disease at home. Polydamas warns Hector that he cannot excel in everything and advises a council; Hector finds his leaders dead or wounded, rebukes Paris, and leads a new charge as Ajax and he exchange threats.","ideas":["The two brother gods pull opposite ends of one 'knot of war'","Idomeneus' taunts over Othryoneus and his boast of descent from Jove through Minos","Menelaus: men tire of sleep, love, song and dance, but the Trojans never of war","Polydamas: the gods give one man war, another counsel"],"terms":[{"t":"Polydamas","d":"Trojan son of Panthous who repeatedly gives Hector cautious advice."},{"t":"Locrian archers","d":"Lightly armed bowmen and slingers under Ajax son of Oileus who shoot from behind the heavy-armed line."}]},{"i":635,"j":664,"loc":"Book XIV (1 of 2)","title":"Agamemnon's despair and the beguiling of Jove","summary":"Nestor leaves the wounded Machaon and meets the wounded kings Agamemnon, Ulysses and Diomed; Agamemnon proposes launching the ships and fleeing by night, and Ulysses rebukes him fiercely for counsel that would ruin the army. Diomed, after reciting his lineage, advises that the wounded kings go near the fight to urge others on, and Neptune in the form of an old man encourages Agamemnon. Juno, resolving to distract Jove, adorns herself, borrows Venus' embroidered girdle under a false pretext, and bribes Sleep with the promise of the Grace Pasithea. On Ida, Jove is overcome with desire, lists his past loves, and lies with her under a golden cloud.","ideas":["Ulysses' scorn for Agamemnon's plan of flight","Diomed's genealogy as a claim to be heard though young","The Deception of Zeus: Juno's toilette, Aphrodite's girdle and the bribing of Sleep","Jove's catalogue of former loves and the flowering bed on Ida"],"terms":[{"t":"Venus' girdle (kestos)","d":"The embroidered band holding love, desire and persuasive flattery, which Juno borrows to seduce Jove."},{"t":"Sleep (Hypnos)","d":"Brother of Death, who fears Jove after once having lulled him for Juno, and is won over by the promise of Pasithea."}]},{"i":665,"j":676,"loc":"Book XIV (2 of 2)","title":"Hector struck down while Jove sleeps","summary":"Sleep tells Neptune that Jove is asleep, and Neptune openly leads the Achaeans, having the best warriors exchange armour for the heaviest shields. Hector's spear is stopped by Ajax's shield-straps, and Ajax strikes him in the neck with a stone, felling him like a lightning-struck oak. His comrades carry him to the Xanthus, where he revives briefly, vomits blood and faints again. The Achaeans press their advantage: Polydamas, Ajax, Acamas and Peneleos trade killings and boasts, Peneleos brandishing the head of Ilioneus, and the Trojans flee as the Muses are invoked to name the slayers.","ideas":["Neptune fights openly at the Achaeans' head while Jove sleeps","Ajax's stone knocks Hector senseless","A chain of revenge killings and vaunts, culminating in Ilioneus' head on a spear","The Trojans routed, the lesser Ajax foremost in pursuit"],"terms":[{"t":"vaunt","d":"The boast a warrior shouts over a fallen enemy, which often provokes the next killing in revenge."}]},{"i":677,"j":708,"loc":"Book XV (1 of 2)","title":"Jove wakes and Apollo restores Hector","summary":"Jove wakes, sees the rout and the stricken Hector, and threatens Juno, recalling how he once hung her from the sky; she swears she did not send Neptune. Jove reveals his plan: Patroclus will kill Sarpedon and be killed by Hector, Achilles will kill Hector, and Troy will fall. On Olympus Juno stirs the gods' resentment, and Minerva restrains Mars from avenging Ascalaphus. Iris orders Neptune off the field; he protests that the world was divided by lot among three brothers but yields. Apollo revives Hector, shakes the aegis to panic the Achaeans, bridges the trench and kicks down the wall like a child's sandcastle, and the Trojans drive their chariots up to the ships.","ideas":["Jove forecasts the deaths of Sarpedon, Patroclus and Hector and the fall of Troy","Neptune's claim to equal honour: sea, underworld and sky allotted to three brothers, earth and Olympus shared","Minerva stops Mars from defying Jove","Apollo destroys the Achaean wall as easily as a child's sand-house"],"terms":[{"t":"aegis","d":"Jove's fringed shield-like emblem, made by Vulcan; when Apollo shakes it, the Achaeans lose heart."},{"t":"Erinyes","d":"Avenging powers which, Iris reminds Neptune, side with the elder brother."}]},{"i":709,"j":735,"loc":"Book XV (2 of 2)","title":"Desperate battle at the ships","summary":"Patroclus leaves the wounded Eurypylus to urge Achilles to fight. The battle at the ships stays level; Ajax kills Caletor as he brings fire, and Teucer's arrows kill Cleitus until Jove snaps his bowstring as he aims at Hector, so Ajax tells him to fight with spear and shield. Hector and Ajax exhort their men, Hector saying it is honourable to die fighting for one's country; Menelaus kills Dolops and Antilochus kills Melanippus. Jove drives Hector on, intending to give him glory until a ship burns, though Hector's death is near; Nestor begs the men to stand, Ajax strides from deck to deck with a great pike, and Hector seizes the stern of Protesilaus' ship and calls for fire.","ideas":["Jove breaks Teucer's bowstring to save Hector","Hector: he who dies fighting for his country dies with honour","Ajax: men who respect each other's opinion are less often killed","Hector grasps the ship of Protesilaus while Ajax alone holds off the fire-bearers"],"terms":[{"t":"aidōs (shame, respect)","d":"The regard for others' opinion that Ajax and Nestor invoke to keep men standing at the ships."}]},{"i":736,"j":762,"loc":"Book XVI (1 of 2)","title":"Patroclus goes out in Achilles' armour","summary":"Patroclus comes weeping to Achilles, reproaches his pitilessness, and asks to lead the Myrmidons in his armour. Achilles, still resentful over the girl Agamemnon took, consents but orders him to return once the ships are saved and not to press on toward Troy. Hector cuts Ajax's spear, fire catches the ship, and Achilles hurries Patroclus into the armour, though not the Pelian spear; the five Myrmidon captains are named, and Achilles prays to Jove of Dodona, who grants the repulse but not Patroclus' safe return. The Myrmidons swarm out like wasps, Patroclus quenches the fire, and the Trojans flee in rout across the trench while he cuts off and slaughters many.","ideas":["Patroclus asks for the armour so the Trojans will take him for Achilles","Achilles' limit: save the ships, then come back","Jove grants half of Achilles' prayer","The ship of Protesilaus burns and is saved; Hector flees across the trench"],"terms":[{"t":"Myrmidons","d":"Achilles' own troops from Phthia, fifty ships under five captains."},{"t":"Xanthus and Balius","d":"Achilles' immortal horses, born of the harpy Podarge to the west wind."}]},{"i":763,"j":791,"loc":"Book XVI (2 of 2)","title":"The deaths of Sarpedon and Patroclus","summary":"Sarpedon confronts Patroclus; Jove considers rescuing his son, but Juno warns that other gods would do the same, so he lets him die and sheds a rain of blood. Patroclus kills Sarpedon, who charges Glaucus to save his armour; Glaucus, healed by Apollo, rallies Hector, and a long fight over the body follows until the Achaeans strip it and Apollo, at Jove's command, has Sleep and Death carry it to Lycia. Ignoring Achilles' warning, Patroclus pursues to the walls, where Apollo thrice repels him. Apollo stuns him and strips his armour, Euphorbus wounds him, and Hector kills him; dying, Patroclus foretells Hector's death at Achilles' hands.","ideas":["Jove yields to fate and lets Sarpedon fall","Sleep and Death bear Sarpedon home to Lycia","Apollo halts Patroclus at the wall: Troy is not his to sack","Patroclus names his killers as fate, Apollo, Euphorbus and only third Hector"],"terms":[{"t":"Sarpedon","d":"Son of Jove and leader of the Lycian allies of Troy."},{"t":"moira (fate)","d":"The appointed lot that even Jove, Juno argues, should not overturn for a mortal."}]},{"i":792,"j":816,"loc":"Book XVII (1 of 2)","title":"Hector takes Achilles' armour","summary":"Menelaus stands over Patroclus' body and kills Euphorbus, who demanded the spoils. Apollo sends Hector against him, and Menelaus, judging he cannot fight a man the gods favour, withdraws to fetch Ajax; Hector strips the armour but retreats before Ajax. Glaucus accuses Hector of cowardice for abandoning Sarpedon's body; Hector replies that Jove's will is stronger than men, puts on Achilles' armour, and Jove pities him as doomed. A fierce fight over the body follows under a darkness Jove has spread: Ajax kills Hippothous, Apollo rouses Aeneas, and Ajax keeps the Achaeans packed tight around the corpse.","ideas":["Menelaus kills Euphorbus, cut down like a young olive tree","Hector puts on Achilles' immortal armour; Jove foresees he will not bring it home","Glaucus threatens that the Lycians will go home","Darkness lies only over the fight around Patroclus"],"terms":[{"t":"spoils","d":"Armour stripped from a fallen enemy, a prize of honour; the struggle here is over both armour and body."}]},{"i":817,"j":843,"loc":"Book XVII (2 of 2)","title":"Rescuing the body of Patroclus","summary":"Both sides tug at the body like men stretching an ox-hide, while Achilles, far off, does not yet know Patroclus is dead. Achilles' immortal horses weep and will not move until Jove, pitying them, gives them strength; Automedon, joined by Alcimedon, kills Aretus and fends off Hector and Aeneas. Minerva, disguised as Phoenix, strengthens Menelaus, Apollo spurs Hector, and Jove turns the battle to the Trojans; Ajax prays that if they must die, it be in daylight, and Jove lifts the mist. Menelaus sends Antilochus weeping to tell Achilles, and he and Meriones carry the body off while the two Ajaxes hold back the Trojans.","ideas":["The weeping horses of Achilles","Ajax's prayer: let us perish, if we must, in daylight","Antilochus is sent with the news to Achilles","Menelaus and Meriones carry the body as the Ajaxes cover the retreat"],"terms":[{"t":"Automedon","d":"Achilles' charioteer, who survives Patroclus and fights on with the immortal horses."}]},{"i":844,"j":873,"loc":"Book XVIII (1 of 2)","title":"Achilles learns of Patroclus and returns","summary":"Antilochus brings Achilles the news that Patroclus has fallen and Hector holds his armour. Achilles collapses in grief; Thetis and the Nereids come from the sea, and when Achilles vows to kill Hector she warns that his own death will follow soon after. Sent by Juno, Iris urges him to show himself at the trench, and with Minerva's fire about his head his triple shout routs the Trojans so the Achaeans can recover the body. That night Hector overrules Polydamas's advice to retreat into the city, while Achilles leads the lament and vows twelve Trojan captives for Patroclus's pyre.","ideas":["Achilles chooses vengeance knowing his death follows Hector's","Achilles curses strife and anger, which taste sweeter than honey","His unarmed shout at the trench saves Patroclus's body","Hector rejects Polydamas's counsel; the Trojans applaud folly"],"terms":[{"t":"Polydamas","d":"Hector's prudent comrade, born the same night, who urges a retreat behind the walls."},{"t":"aegis","d":"Minerva's tasselled shield-cloak, here flung over Achilles' shoulders."}]},{"i":874,"j":893,"loc":"Book XVIII (2 of 2)","title":"Vulcan forges the Shield of Achilles","summary":"Thetis visits Vulcan's bronze house on Olympus, where he is building self-moving tripods attended by golden handmaids. Remembering how she sheltered him after his fall, he agrees to make new armour for her doomed son. The book then describes the Shield of Achilles: earth, sky, sea and stars; a city at peace with weddings and a lawsuit over blood-money; a city at war with ambush and battle; ploughing, harvest, vintage, cattle attacked by lions, a sheep pasture and a dance floor, all ringed by Oceanus. Vulcan adds breastplate, helmet and greaves, and Thetis carries them down from Olympus.","ideas":["Vulcan repays Thetis's old kindness","The Shield shows the whole ordered world in peace and war","A city of justice and festival set beside a city under siege","Farm labour, song and dance framed by the river Oceanus"],"terms":[{"t":"Shield of Achilles","d":"The five-layered shield whose decorated scenes are described at length in this book."},{"t":"blood-money","d":"Payment for a killing, disputed before elders in the shield's peaceful city."}]},{"i":894,"j":915,"loc":"Book XIX (1 of 2)","title":"Achilles and Agamemnon are reconciled","summary":"At dawn Thetis brings the armour and preserves Patroclus's body with nectar and ambrosia. Achilles calls an assembly and renounces his anger; Agamemnon blames Jove, Fate and Erinys, and tells how Folly once deceived Jove himself at the birth of Hercules and Eurystheus. Achilles wants to fight at once, but Ulysses insists the army must eat first and that the gifts and an oath be given properly. Agamemnon swears he never touched Briseis, sacrifices a boar, and the gifts are carried to Achilles' ships.","ideas":["Achilles puts away his wrath for the sake of revenge","Agamemnon attributes his offence to Folly (Atē) sent by the gods","Ulysses argues that fighting men need food","The promised gifts and Briseis are restored under oath"],"terms":[{"t":"Folly (Atē)","d":"Eldest daughter of Jove, who blinds men's minds and whom Jove hurled from heaven."},{"t":"Erinyes","d":"Powers below who punish false oaths, invoked by Agamemnon."}]},{"i":916,"j":926,"loc":"Book XIX (2 of 2)","title":"Briseis mourns; Achilles arms for battle","summary":"Briseis throws herself on Patroclus's body and recalls his kindness to her after her husband and brothers were killed. Achilles refuses food, grieving for Patroclus more than he would for his father or his son Neoptolemus, so Jove sends Minerva to sustain him with nectar and ambrosia. He puts on Vulcan's armour, which gleams like a beacon, and takes his father's Pelian ash spear. When he rebukes his horses, Xanthus is given speech by Juno and foretells his death at the hands of a god and a man; Achilles accepts it and drives into battle.","ideas":["Briseis's lament for the gentle Patroclus","Achilles fasts in grief and is fed by Minerva","The arming of Achilles in the divine armour","The horse Xanthus prophesies Achilles' death"],"terms":[{"t":"Pelian ash","d":"The huge spear from Mt. Pelion, given by Chiron to Peleus, that only Achilles can wield."},{"t":"Xanthus and Balius","d":"Achilles' immortal horses, offspring of Podarge."}]},{"i":927,"j":942,"loc":"Book XX (1 of 2)","title":"The gods take sides for battle","summary":"Jove summons all the gods to council and frees them to help whichever side they wish, fearing Achilles might otherwise storm Troy beyond fate. Juno, Minerva, Neptune, Mercury and Vulcan join the Achaeans; Mars, Apollo, Diana, Leto, Xanthus and Venus join the Trojans. Their uproar shakes the earth so that Hades fears his realm will be laid bare. Apollo, disguised as Lycaon, goads Aeneas to face Achilles; Neptune persuades Juno that the gods should watch from the barrow of Hercules, while the Trojan gods sit on Callicolone.","ideas":["Jove releases the gods into the war","Cosmic upheaval as gods face gods","Apollo stirs Aeneas against Achilles","The divine factions withdraw to watch from two hills"],"terms":[{"t":"Themis","d":"Goddess who summons the gods to Jove's council."},{"t":"Callicolone","d":"Hill where the pro-Trojan gods sit to watch the fighting."}]},{"i":943,"j":964,"loc":"Book XX (2 of 2)","title":"Aeneas is rescued; Achilles' rampage","summary":"Achilles taunts Aeneas, who answers with his lineage from Dardanus through Tros and Assaracus. Aeneas's spear is stopped by the gold layer of the divine shield; as he lifts a huge stone, Neptune, saying it is fated that Aeneas and his descendants will rule the Trojans, hides him in mist and carries him away. Hector is warned off by Apollo, but after Achilles kills his young brother Polydorus he attacks; Minerva turns his spear back and Apollo snatches him away. Achilles then cuts down many Trojans, including the suppliant Tros, until his chariot is spattered with blood.","ideas":["Aeneas recites the Dardanian genealogy","Neptune saves Aeneas, destined to rule the Trojans","Achilles kills Polydorus; Apollo saves Hector","Achilles rages like a forest fire, sparing no suppliant"],"terms":[{"t":"Dardanus","d":"Son of Jove and founder of Dardania, ancestor of both Priam's and Aeneas's lines."}]},{"i":965,"j":988,"loc":"Book XXI (1 of 2)","title":"Achilles fights the river Scamander","summary":"Achilles splits the Trojans at the ford of Xanthus, slaughters many in the river and takes twelve youths alive for Patroclus's pyre. He kills Lycaon, whom he had once sold into Lemnos, rejecting his plea since quarter ended with Patroclus's death, and kills Asteropaeus, grandson of the river Axius, boasting that Jove's descendants outmatch any river. The river, choked with corpses, rises against him and chases him across the plain until Neptune and Minerva reassure him. When Scamander calls on Simois to bury Achilles in silt, Juno sends Vulcan, whose fire burns the plain and boils the river until he swears to stop helping Troy.","ideas":["Achilles refuses Lycaon's supplication","Achilles claims descent from Jove over Asteropaeus's river lineage","The river god nearly drowns Achilles","Fire against water: Vulcan subdues Scamander"],"terms":[{"t":"Scamander / Xanthus","d":"The river of Troy, called Xanthus by gods and Scamander by men, here fighting as a god."}]},{"i":989,"j":1005,"loc":"Book XXI (2 of 2)","title":"The gods fight; Trojans flee inside","summary":"The other gods fall on each other while Jove laughs: Minerva fells Mars with a boundary stone and strikes down Venus, Apollo declines to fight Neptune over short-lived mortals, and Juno beats Diana with her own bow; Mercury yields to Leto. Apollo enters Troy to guard its wall while the rest return to Olympus. Priam orders the gates opened for the fleeing Trojans. Apollo spurs Agenor to stand against Achilles, then snatches him away and, in Agenor's likeness, decoys Achilles toward the river while the Trojans crowd into the city.","ideas":["The battle of the gods, half comic","Apollo: men are like leaves that grow and fall","Neptune recalls Laomedon's broken bargain","Apollo's decoy lets the Trojans escape inside"],"terms":[{"t":"theomachy","d":"The battle among the gods themselves, fought in this section."}]},{"i":1006,"j":1021,"loc":"Book XXII (1 of 2)","title":"Hector waits; the chase round Troy","summary":"Apollo reveals his trick and Achilles races back toward the city, gleaming like the Dog Star. Priam and Hecuba beg Hector from the wall to come inside, Priam picturing the city's fall and his own death, Hecuba baring the breast that nursed him. Hector, ashamed to face Polydamas and the Trojans after his folly, weighs offering Helen and half of Troy's wealth but judges parley impossible. When Achilles arrives, fear seizes him and he runs; the two circle the walls three times past the springs while Jove asks whether to save him and Minerva objects.","ideas":["Priam's and Hecuba's pleas fail to move Hector","Hector's soliloquy: shame forbids retreat","The pursuit past the hot and cold springs","Jove pities Hector but yields to Minerva"],"terms":[{"t":"Scaean gates","d":"The gates of Troy before which Hector stands to await Achilles."}]},{"i":1022,"j":1044,"loc":"Book XXII (2 of 2)","title":"The death of Hector and Troy's laments","summary":"Jove weighs the dooms in his golden scales, Hector's sinks, and Apollo leaves him. Minerva, disguised as Deiphobus, persuades Hector to stand; Achilles refuses his pact to respect the dead, and when Hector's spear fails and Deiphobus has vanished, Hector sees the gods' deceit and charges. Achilles spears him through the throat, refuses his dying plea for ransom, and hears him foretell his death by Paris and Apollo. Achilles drags the body behind his chariot; Priam and Hecuba lament, and Andromache faints on the wall and mourns the orphaned Astyanax.","ideas":["Jove's scales decide Hector's doom","Minerva's deception; Hector resolves to die gloriously","Achilles refuses burial and rejects every ransom","Andromache's lament for Hector and Astyanax"],"terms":[{"t":"Astyanax","d":"Hector's infant son, named because his father alone defended the city."}]},{"i":1045,"j":1065,"loc":"Book XXIII (1 of 3)","title":"Patroclus's ghost and his funeral","summary":"The Myrmidons drive three times round Patroclus's body and Achilles throws Hector's corpse in the dust beside the bier. Asleep on the shore, Achilles is visited by Patroclus's ghost, who asks for quick burial so he may pass the gates of Hades and that their bones share one urn; Achilles tries to embrace him and the spirit vanishes. Next day wood is brought from Ida, Achilles cuts the lock vowed to the Spercheius, and the pyre burns horses, dogs and twelve Trojan youths once Iris summons Boreas and Zephyrus. Venus and Apollo protect Hector's body, and Patroclus's bones are placed in a golden urn under a modest barrow.","ideas":["Patroclus's ghost asks for burial and a shared urn","The dead in Hades are phantoms without life","Human sacrifice and offerings on the pyre","Gods keep Hector's body from harm"],"terms":[{"t":"psychē (spirit)","d":"The ghost of Patroclus, like him in voice and form but impossible to embrace."}]},{"i":1066,"j":1092,"loc":"Book XXIII (2 of 3)","title":"The chariot race at the funeral games","summary":"Achilles sets out prizes and holds aloof himself, since his immortal horses mourn their driver. Nestor gives Antilochus a long lesson in skill at the turning-post. In the race Apollo makes Diomed drop his whip, Minerva restores it and breaks Eumelus's yoke, and Antilochus forces past Menelaus at a narrow spot; Idomeneus and Ajax son of Oileus quarrel over who leads. Diomed wins; when Achilles proposes second prize for the fallen Eumelus, Antilochus objects, and Menelaus demands an oath until Antilochus yields the mare and Menelaus gives it back. Achilles gives the unclaimed urn to old Nestor, who recalls his own youthful victories.","ideas":["Nestor: skill (mētis) beats speed","Gods interfere in the race","The dispute between Menelaus and Antilochus settled by grace","Nestor honoured with a prize in his old age"],"terms":[{"t":"mētis (cunning skill)","d":"Craft and know-how, which Nestor says lets a driver beat faster horses."},{"t":"doubling-post","d":"The turning-mark of the course, a dead tree stump with two white stones."}]},{"i":1093,"j":1111,"loc":"Book XXIII (3 of 3)","title":"Boxing, wrestling and the other contests","summary":"Epeus wins the boxing by knocking out Euryalus. Ajax son of Telamon and Ulysses wrestle to a draw, Ulysses using a trick at the back of the knee, and Achilles divides the prize. In the foot-race Ulysses prays to Minerva, Ajax son of Oileus slips in dung, and Antilochus earns extra gold for a compliment to Achilles. Ajax and Diomed fight in armour until the onlookers stop them, Polypoetes outthrows the others with the iron quoit, Meriones beats Teucer at archery after vowing a hecatomb to Apollo, and Achilles gives Agamemnon the javelin prize without a contest.","ideas":["Strength against cunning in the wrestling","Minerva favours Ulysses in the foot-race","Achilles acts as a gracious, fair-minded host","Prayer to Apollo decides the archery"],"terms":[]},{"i":1112,"j":1135,"loc":"Book XXIV (1 of 3)","title":"The gods decide Hector must be ransomed","summary":"Sleepless with grief, Achilles drags Hector round Patroclus's tomb each dawn, but Apollo shields the body. On the twelfth day Apollo rebukes the gods for abandoning Hector; Juno objects, and Jove decrees that Achilles shall accept a ransom. Thetis tells Achilles, who agrees, and Iris tells Priam to go alone to the ships with gifts, promising Mercury as escort. Over Hecuba's protest Priam gathers treasure, berates his surviving sons, and after a libation Jove sends an eagle on the right as a sign.","ideas":["Apollo condemns Achilles' outrage on the dead","Jove arranges the ransom through Thetis and Iris","Priam resolves to face his son's killer","The eagle omen confirms the journey"],"terms":[{"t":"ransom (apoina)","d":"Gifts offered to recover a captive or a body, here Priam's treasure for Hector."}]},{"i":1136,"j":1160,"loc":"Book XXIV (2 of 3)","title":"Priam supplicates Achilles","summary":"Mercury, in the form of a young Myrmidon squire, meets Priam on the plain, assures him Hector's body is unharmed, puts the guards to sleep and brings him to Achilles' hut before revealing himself. Priam clasps Achilles' knees and kisses his hands, urging him to think of his own father; both weep, Priam for Hector and Achilles for Peleus and Patroclus. Achilles speaks of Jove's two urns of good and evil gifts and of Peleus's fortune, then has the body washed and laid on the waggon. They eat together as Niobe once did, marvel at each other, and Achilles grants a truce for Hector's funeral.","ideas":["Priam kisses the hands that killed his sons","Shared grief: Priam's sorrow mirrors Peleus's","Jove's two urns of good and evil","Niobe's example; a truce of eleven days"],"terms":[{"t":"two urns of Jove","d":"Achilles' image of the jars from which Jove mixes good and evil lots for men."},{"t":"suppliant","d":"One who clasps another's knees seeking mercy, protected by Jove."}]},{"i":1161,"j":1169,"loc":"Book XXIV (3 of 3)","title":"Hector's return, laments and funeral","summary":"Mercury wakes Priam in the night and drives him unseen out of the camp. Cassandra sees him from Pergamus and calls the city to meet Hector's body. Andromache laments that Hector died young and fears for Astyanax and the city; Hecuba marvels that his body lies fresh as dew; Helen mourns the one brother-in-law who was always kind to her. For nine days the Trojans gather wood, burn Hector on the tenth, bury his bones in a golden urn under a barrow, and feast in Priam's house; the poem ends with the funeral of Hector tamer of horses.","ideas":["Cassandra first sees the returning body","Three laments: Andromache, Hecuba, Helen","Hector's pyre, urn and barrow","The poem closes on Hector's funeral"],"terms":[{"t":"Pergamus","d":"The citadel of Troy, from which Cassandra sees Priam returning."}]}]});